This repo contains the configuration that describes the content of the
`qcom-next` integration kernel tree, plus the CI that builds and publishes it.

qcom-next is a function of its configuration:

    qcom-next = base.sha + topics[].sha merged in order

`qcom-next` is never hand-edited. The only way to change it is to change
`qcom-next.yaml` through a reviewed pull request on this repository.


Source of truth
---------------

`qcom-next.yaml` records, for every consumed topic branch, the exact SHA that
was validated, the tag pinning that SHA, and the upstream baseline the topics
are merged on top of.

`qcom-next.conf`, the flat three-column file automerge's `ci-merge` reads, is
generated from the YAML on every run and thrown away. Do not edit it by hand.

Topic branch owners live in `topic_maintainers.json`, keyed by the topic `name`
used in `qcom-next.yaml`.


Promoting a topic branch
------------------------

1. Tag the SHA you want consumed, on your topic branch:

       <branch>-<kernel-version>-<YYYYMMDD>

   for example `tech/bsp/clk-7.3-rc1-20260907`. Tagging matters: without a tag
   the consumed commit becomes unreachable the moment the topic branch is
   rebased.

2. Open a pull request against this repository that changes your topic's `sha`
   and `tag` in `qcom-next.yaml`.

3. CI builds the tree those pins describe, with every other topic held at its
   current SHA, and pushes it to `qualcomm-linux/kernel` as
   `qcom-next-candidate-<pr>`. It then runs `pre_merge` and `pre_merge_yocto`
   against that branch and reports both on the PR.

4. When both pass, the integrator merges the PR. That force-pushes the exact
   tested commit to `qcom-next` and deletes the candidate branch.

If CI/CD fails, push a fix. The candidate branch is rebuilt in place, so refs
do not accumulate.

Only a change to `qcom-next.yaml` starts any of this. Other kernel-config pull
requests are unaffected.


Upreving the kernel RC
----------------------

An RC uprev changes only the `base` block and leaves every topic SHA untouched,
so a regression is attributable to upstream rather than to a topic update. It
goes through the same PR flow.


What CI does
------------

    .github/workflows/qcom-next.yml          on a qcom-next.yaml PR
      scripts/qcom_next_tree.sh                build the tree
        scripts/qcom_next_conf.py                YAML -> ci-merge conf
      push qcom-next-candidate-<pr>            to qualcomm-linux/kernel
      scripts/run_cicd.sh                      pre_merge, pre_merge_yocto

    .github/workflows/qcom-next-publish.yml  when that PR is merged
      force-push the candidate to qcom-next, delete the candidate branch

Two properties the scripts enforce rather than assume:

  * After the merge, every pinned SHA must be an ancestor of the result. If
    pinning had silently fallen back to a branch tip, the pinned commit would
    not be there, and the run fails.

  * Before publishing, every pin in the *merged* `qcom-next.yaml` must be in
    the candidate. A candidate built against an older base is refused rather
    than published.

The generated tree carries `qcom-next/topic_SHA1` and `qcom-next/merge.log`, so
a published `qcom-next` states which topic SHAs it is built from and how the
merge went. Credentials are redacted from the log before it is committed.


Running it locally
------------------

Needs `pyyaml` and a checkout of automerge next to this repo:

    pip install pyyaml

Build the tree from the current config:

    scripts/qcom_next_tree.sh \
        --kernel-dir /path/to/kernel-topics \
        --automerge  /path/to/automerge \
        --out-dir    /tmp/qcom-next-out

Inspect what the config projects to, without building:

    scripts/qcom_next_conf.py pins
    scripts/qcom_next_conf.py conf --local-repo /path/to/kernel-topics

Run the offline tests, which build small fixture repositories and exercise the
real scripts:

    scripts/selftest_qcom_next.sh


Conflicts
---------

Conflicts are replayed from the rerere cache named in `rerere_cache`. If a
merge hits a conflict with no recorded resolution, the run fails and names the
topic. Resolve it locally with the same config and cache revision, push the
updated rerere cache, and re-run.


Migration helper
----------------

`scripts/bootstrap_qcom_next_yaml.py` generated `qcom-next.yaml` from the
legacy `qcom-next.conf`, taking the SHAs from the `topic_SHA1` table published
alongside a generated qcom-next. It is kept in-tree so the migration is
reproducible; it is not part of the normal promotion flow.


Other files
-----------

`ci.conf.sample` is a sample automerge configuration if you want to run local
integration testing with your own branch list.
